‘I will not tolerate such a demeaning act on her part,’ he said, ‘nor such insolence from you. Never shall it be said that I allowed such infamy: my wife’s children would never be able to mix in Germany’s noble chapters. No, my sister will marry no one but a baron of the Empire.’

Cunégonde threw herself at his feet and bathed them with her tears; he was inflexible.

‘You great numskull,’ said Candide, ‘I’ve saved you from the galleys, I’ve paid your ransom, and I’ve paid your sister’s. She was washing dishes here, she’s ugly, I have the goodness to make her my wife, and you still think you’re going to stand in our way! I’d kill you all over again if I let my anger have its way.’

‘You can kill me all over again if you want,’ said the Baron, ‘but you won’t marry my sister so long as I live.’


